UML selftests
=============

Host-runnable tests that exercise a built User Mode Linux binary: boot,
process management, memory faults, signal frames, TLS across fork,
syscall restart, module load/unload, and a syscall/fault
microbenchmark.  Every test runs in both UML userspace modes (ptrace
and seccomp) and reports TAP, skipping cleanly when a mode or feature
is unavailable.

Quick start
-----------

Build a UML kernel (any recent x86_64 host):

    make ARCH=um defconfig
    ./scripts/config --file .config -m TEST_LKM	 # for the module test
    make ARCH=um olddefconfig
    make ARCH=um -j$(nproc) linux modules

Build and run the suite against it:

    cd tools/testing/selftests/um
    make
    UML_BIN=/path/to/build/linux ./um_boot_test.sh
    UML_BIN=/path/to/build/linux ./um_core_test.sh
    UML_BIN=/path/to/build/linux ./um_module_test.sh
    UML_BIN=/path/to/build/linux ./um_bench_test.sh

or through the kselftest runner:

    make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=um run_tests \
	UML_BIN=/path/to/build/linux

The `config` fragment in this directory lists the kernel options the
full suite wants (only CONFIG_HOSTFS is a hard requirement; everything
else degrades to TAP SKIP).

Mechanism
---------

Each test boots $UML_BIN with a static payload binary as init on a
hostfs root pointing at a scratch directory.  The payload writes its
per-subtest results to /result.tap, which lands in the scratch
directory on the host; the runner relays them as TAP.  Success is the
payload's completion marker plus a boot log free of kernel
diagnostics -- never the UML process's exit code, which proves nothing.

Knobs (environment): UML_BIN (required), UML_MODES ("ptrace seccomp"),
UML_MEM, UML_TIMEOUT, UML_EXTRA_ARGS, UML_TEST_KO (module test),
UML_BENCH_BOOTS / UML_WRAP (benchmark; use UML_WRAP="taskset -c ..."
to pin on heterogeneous-core machines).

ARM64 (or other foreign-arch) UML
---------------------------------

qemu-user cannot host UML -- it emulates neither ptrace of guest
children nor guest seccomp filters, which are exactly the mechanisms
UML's process model is built from.  um_arm64_rig.sh instead boots a
real arm64 kernel under qemu-system-aarch64 (TCG) and runs the UML
binary and the same payloads inside it, extracting one TAP stream from
the serial console.  It needs qemu-system-aarch64, an arm64 guest
kernel Image, a static arm64 busybox, and payloads cross-built with
`make CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-`; see the header of
um_arm64_rig.sh for the exact knobs.  Do not benchmark under TCG: the
numbers measure qemu.
